WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:02.610
- The Feds are investigating

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:06.030
one of the largest drug
busts in US history.

00:00:06.030 --> 00:00:09.990
- In June, 2019, news
breaks of a massive seizure

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.370
in the port of Philadelphia.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:13.680 position:58%
20 tons of drugs in a single seizure.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.750
- Authorities say the drugs
could have a street value

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:17.940
of more than 1 billion dollars.

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:21.720
- This was not like just any
other drug trafficking case.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.920
Yes, drugs are smuggled on
container ships every day,

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:29.430
but this one was extraordinary
and this one was different.

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:32.070
It was not only the
sheer quantity of drugs,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:36.030
it was also the level of
involvement of the crew.

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.250 position:64%
- This wasn't just happening on one ship.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.180
The cartel's penetration
of MSC's crewing apparatus

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:46.560
was so deep that they
could run multiple crews

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.650
on multiple ships with
these massive loads of drugs

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:50.850
that were going to Europe.

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.860
It really has created this kind of

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.860
gangland feel that overlays
global container shipping.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.690
- The fact that there's the
world's largest shipping company

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.220
that's at the center of this,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.070 position:56%
that makes it a very sensitive topic

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.980 position:66%
and also one that could have ramifications

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.723
for the wider shipping industry.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.370
There is this really interesting

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.350
and problematic tension

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.320
just in the nature of
the globalized economy.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.960 position:61%
Most people don't give a second thought

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.260
to the way that their goods arrive.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.020
It all arrives on these
giant massive vessels

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.360
that are sailing around the world.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:40.860
- 90% of global trade is conducted
on ships, and every year,

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:45.120
14 dollars trillion of goods
are moved around the world.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.700
- The shipping industry is
essentially the backbone

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:50.760 position:59%
of globalization, of a global economy.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:55.410
What that means is that
there's this pressure on ports

00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.710
and on shipping companies
and on customs authorities

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.400 position:64%
to ensure that global trade runs smoothly

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.650
and that it runs quickly.

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:09.330
Every time a container is opened,
that slows down the trade.

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:13.320
- It also prevents security
services, customs officials,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.230
and others from really digging in

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:18.210
and examining the containers

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.703
as they move through this system.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:30.500
- The ports of Northern Europe,
primarily Antwerp in Belgium

00:02:30.870 --> 00:02:32.910
and Rotterdam in the Netherlands

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.120 position:59%
have long been central to global trade

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.580 position:66%
dating all the way back to the spice trade

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.200
hundreds of years ago.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:44.550
As a result, the infrastructure
built up around these ports

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:48.540
has been increasingly
efficient and spectacular.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:52.410
As the market for cocaine
has grown in Europe,

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:54.360 position:61%
it only makes sense to be shipping them

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.300 position:58%
through these two ports in the north.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.640 position:64%
That gives the traffickers an opportunity

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:02.790 position:59%
to not necessarily service the markets

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:07.410
in the Netherlands or in
Belgium, but all across Europe

00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.950
and even down into
Africa and Asia as well.

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:14.580
We visited one of these law
enforcement customs stations

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.605 position:61%
where they scan cargo that they believe

00:03:18.605 --> 00:03:20.790
contains illicit goods.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:25.200
On this trip, just a regular
day in the port of Antwerp,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.770
customs officials had pulled
aside a shipping container.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:30.630
It had a high risk level,

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.490
and they put it through the scanner

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:33.840
and ended up searching it.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:37.260 position:59%
It was cocoa beans from South America.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.210
Upon searching the container,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.783
they found almost a
thousand kilos of cocaine.

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:48.070
- We have several
examples of where they are

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:54.000
use all their creativity
to get things past us.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:57.180
I'm going to show you an
image of import of a Cadillac,

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:59.880
a pink Cadillac, and
then you see in the door

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:03.360
of the Cadillac all kind
of strange packages.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:08.360
So when we examined it, it was
ecstasy, 50 kilos of ecstasy.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:11.940
As you see here, they filled

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:14.370 position:66%
like the shell of pineapples with cocaine.

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.640
This one is an import
of teak from Ecuador.

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.700 position:61%
The left pile of wood had darker edges.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.043 position:66%
The cocaine was hidden in the wood itself.

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.320
And also, the structure of
the container and truck.

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.570 position:64%
Sometimes they hid stuff in the structure

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:32.460
like in the floor or the ceiling.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.640 position:64%
- On a yearly basis, we are talking about

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.110 position:66%
40 to 50,000 containers which are scanned.

00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:43.950
The number of containers
entering and leaving the port

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:47.100 position:56%
are millions of millions, and today,

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.280 position:64%
we do not have the infrastructure to scan

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:51.630
all the containers.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:56.630
We scan 1.5% of the containers
entering the port of Antwerp.

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.170
We stop a lot of tons, but
if you look to the outcome,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.050
the effect is zero.

00:05:04.050 --> 00:05:07.230 position:64%
There is still huge quantities of cocaine

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.750
entering the European market.

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:13.470
- For customs officials,
they're essentially inundated

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.380 position:63%
with this problem and really outmatched,

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.200
and that's something actually
that the traffickers know

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:22.050 position:63%
and realize and have taken advantage of.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:24.990 position:61%
For them to lose a thousand kilos here,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:28.170
a thousand kilos there, is
not the end of the world

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:29.400
because they just expect

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:32.670
that they're just going
to continue shipping more.

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:37.670
Around 2008, 2009, there
was kind of a new phenomenon

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.830
that police officials,
customs authorities,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:43.080
started noticing.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.180
These large commercial ships,

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.930
before they entered the
ports, crew on board the ship

00:05:48.930 --> 00:05:53.370
would actually toss over
bags filled with cocaine.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.950
One of their collaborators
or co-conspirators

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.920
would come up in a smaller
kind of fishing vessel

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.660
and pick up the cocaine.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.380 position:58%
And this was known as "the drop-off".

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:06.690 position:61%
And that was a really big breakthrough.

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:10.870
What this meant was that
there was somebody on the ship

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.560
that was in on it.

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:16.410 position:64%
As European law enforcement investigators

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:18.960 position:64%
started kind of honing in on this problem

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.120
of commercial shipping companies

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:24.900
being at the center of
the global cocaine trade,

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.410
they started finding patterns,
putting together pieces,

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:31.410
and they realized that there
was one shipping company

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.620
that seemed to be at the center
of a lot of these drop-offs,

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:35.973
and that was MSC.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.920
Mediterranean Shipping Company
is known by its initials MSC.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.950
They are today the world's
largest shipping company.

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.230 position:59%
- MSC is a really interesting company.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:52.140 position:58%
Over a period of five or six decades,

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:55.410
They go from one ship
to a fleet now of 650

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:57.060
surpassing everybody.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.920 position:58%
It's a private company in Switzerland

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:02.310
so its finances in
particular are pretty opaque.

00:07:02.310 --> 00:07:05.160
It does sort of make
the miracle that MSC is

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:06.990 position:59%
in terms of the empire that they built

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:08.490
over a short period of time

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:11.490
all the more intriguing and
all the more mysterious.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:15.131
- Starting in the early 2000s,

00:07:15.131 --> 00:07:18.000
MSC became a major player

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:21.900
in the sea lanes from
South America to Europe.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.440
They started investing heavily
in what are called "reefers"

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:30.060
or refrigerated containers
to move refrigerated goods.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.890
- That turned out to
be a golden opportunity

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:36.090
for the traffickers because you
basically could put the coke

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:39.960
inside containers of blueberries
or strawberries or bananas

00:07:39.960 --> 00:07:42.000
and it would move quickly
through the system,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:43.500 position:64%
and they don't wanna like slow those down

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.610
because the fruit is a perishable
commodity, into Europe.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.650
- The cooling compartment
is a very popular place

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:52.620
to hide cocaine because that
compartment can be opened

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.710
and closed without breaking
the seal of the container.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:57.960 position:64%
- One of the key moments in the build out

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:01.020
of this infrastructure was the
expansion of the Panama Canal

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:02.070
in 2016.

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.450
So you've got much bigger ships,

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:05.040
much greater amounts of cargo.

00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.800
Again, much of it fruit
and vegetables, meats,

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:12.330
other kinds of commodities,
but that expansion around 2016

00:08:12.330 --> 00:08:15.900
basically correlates with a
big jump in cocaine seizures

00:08:15.900 --> 00:08:18.510 position:59%
in the ports of Antwerp and Rotterdam.

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:22.650
- Because MSC dominated
this particular route,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.820 position:66%
they became an easy target by traffickers.

00:08:26.820 --> 00:08:29.910
Law enforcement
investigators started seeing

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:33.570
that cocaine that had
made its way to Europe

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:37.620 position:61%
had actually stopped through a US port.

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.170
So they started kind of
working backwards at first,

00:08:40.170 --> 00:08:43.500
thinking if all of this
cocaine is ending up in Europe

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:47.760
but it's stopping in the US,
how did we not detect it?

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:51.600
- At the same time, European
authorities are also

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.990
conducting investigations
where they're coming across

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:57.480
the infiltration of other parts of

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:00.300
the shipping industry
generally, but especially MSC.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.360
- So over the course
of two to three years,

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:07.890 position:63%
they started following about four ships,

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:11.880
and that was the MSC
Desiree, the MSC Carlotta,

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:16.350
the MSC Avni, and the MSC Gayane.

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:19.950
The MSC Gayane is a monstrous ship.

00:09:19.950 --> 00:09:23.880
This is a ship that is the
length of three football fields.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.320
It's one of the biggest ships
sailing the oceans today.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.530
- MSC Gayane was following
a particular route

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:33.960 position:56%
from the west coast of South America

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:36.900
through the Panama Canal
up to Philadelphia,

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:39.270
and from there, from the east
coast of the United States

00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:42.210
over to Rotterdam and Antwerp.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:46.050 position:63%
- Gayane set sail in the spring of 2019.

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:50.340
In mid-June after the Gayane
had been at sea for many weeks,

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:53.910
it pulled into the mouth
of the Delaware Bay.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:54.810
This day,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.140
instead of the Gayane being
met by a riverboat pilot,

00:09:58.140 --> 00:10:02.520
they were met by the Coast
Guard, Philadelphia Police,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.620
Homeland Security officials,

00:10:04.620 --> 00:10:06.690 position:63%
customs and border protection officials.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:10.500
There were over a hundred law
enforcement agents waiting.

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:13.350
They corralled the crew,
start questioning them,

00:10:13.350 --> 00:10:16.860
swabbing their hands for
any traces of cocaine.

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:18.900
Those came back positive.

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.830
They started doing a deeper
inspection on the containers

00:10:22.830 --> 00:10:27.240
and eventually found kind of
their first hit of cocaine

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.440
and then they found more

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:30.573
and they found more

00:10:30.573 --> 00:10:33.060 position:63%
and eventually came up with the 20 tons.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:37.170
Initially, law enforcement
investigators were stumped.

00:10:37.170 --> 00:10:40.830
How did they get so much
cocaine on this ship?

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.180
So what they came to find out
through interviewing the crews

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.700
and through other kind
of physical evidence

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.520 position:59%
that they were able to obtain was that

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:54.480
the cocaine was actually
loaded onto the ship

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:57.600
while it was in the open
ocean of South America.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:01.140
As the Gayane was powering
through the open ocean,

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:05.190
in the middle of the night,
small boats pulled up alongside

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:10.050
and essentially loaded
the cocaine that way.

00:11:10.050 --> 00:11:14.490
The crew members used
the crane of the Gayane

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:18.150
and basically hoisted up large nets

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:22.200
filled with the bales of
cocaine onto the cargo ship.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.630
Then they would take that cocaine

00:11:24.630 --> 00:11:26.730
and then they pushed it
into some of the containers

00:11:26.730 --> 00:11:29.670 position:58%
and basically stashed it on the ship.

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:33.000 position:63%
So it was an extremely brazen operation.

00:11:33.000 --> 00:11:35.880
- They do this loading
at sea not just once,

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:36.930
but multiple times.

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.880
They load it as it's going down

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:40.470
the west coast of South America,

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.560
turns around at a port in
Chile, and then goes back up,

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.810
and they do it several
times on the way back up.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:47.910
There were eight crew
members in all on the Gayane

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.620
that was involved in
the smuggling operation

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:51.000
including the chief mate,

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:53.760
and the chief mate plays a
really key role on the ship.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.370
He puts together the schedules,
for example, of the crewman,

00:11:56.370 --> 00:12:00.240 position:56%
so he can get the crew that he knows

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.060
are in on this operation
working, for example,

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:04.110
all at night

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:06.960
when he needs them to be
there to load the cocaine.

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.950
He also has a lot of information
about the stowage plans,

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:13.380 position:56%
so which containers are going where.

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.570
US authorities did not arrest
the captain of the ship,

00:12:15.570 --> 00:12:17.190
but you don't need the captain.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:18.300
But for an operation like this,

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:20.550
you do need a very senior officer

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:23.610
who can control the movement
of the crew, the ship,

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:25.260
and all sorts of things.

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:26.700
And keep in mind that

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:28.980 position:61%
this wasn't just happening on one ship.

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:30.960 position:58%
US authorities had also made seizures

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.270
on the Carlotta and the Desiree

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:35.970
which they believed operated
in exactly the same way.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.000
This is something that
was by 2019, June of 2019,

00:12:39.000 --> 00:12:41.220
it was a pretty well-oiled system,

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:42.930
so that's why they were confident

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:45.420 position:58%
they could move that that many drugs,

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:48.810 position:64%
that tonnage of cocaine on a single load.

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:51.570
- In total, there were
about eight crew members

00:12:51.570 --> 00:12:55.860
that were implicated in
the Gayane smuggling,

00:12:55.860 --> 00:13:00.860
and of those eight, four of
them had in fact been recruited

00:13:01.110 --> 00:13:03.090 position:61%
before ever stepping foot on the Gayane

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:05.130
on that particular voyage.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.930 position:61%
At least one of them had been recruited

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.230 position:56%
in their home country of Montenegro.

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:14.520
- Montenegro has a long
history of seafarers.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.230 position:56%
The country's right on the Adriatic,

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:19.650
produces a lot of sailors
and crewmen and officers

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.510 position:56%
who are very skilled at what they do

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:24.010
and they've been plying
the oceans for centuries.

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:28.020 position:64%
MSC taps into that in a way that's bigger

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:29.190
than any other shipping company.

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:33.300
They employ basically a
third or more than a third

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:34.803
of all Montenegrin sailors.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.990
- Organized criminal groups

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:43.620 position:61%
are particularly strong in the Balkans.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:47.190
There's a large network
of individuals and groups

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.110 position:64%
that have been operating there for years,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:52.590
really in the wake of
the breakup of Yugoslavia

00:13:52.590 --> 00:13:54.330
and the Balkan Wars.

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:58.623
As the economy there collapsed,
it beget a lot of crime.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:02.610
If you're a cartel, if
you're trafficking drugs,

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:06.012
it only made sense and
it was easy for them

00:14:06.012 --> 00:14:08.370
to target not only sailors,

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:10.713
but to target sailors on MSC ships.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.930 position:61%
- Senior law enforcement officials from

00:14:15.930 --> 00:14:20.190
at least three countries
approached MSC management

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:22.320
and said, look this is
what the problem is.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:25.860
They had been told that their
crew were being recruited

00:14:25.860 --> 00:14:27.150
by the Balkan cartel

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:30.540
and so from the point of view
of law enforcement in Europe

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.700 position:64%
and ultimately law enforcement in the US,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.970
MSC had multiple opportunities
to try and fix this problem

00:14:35.970 --> 00:14:39.660
before 2019 and didn't
do enough to do that.

00:14:39.660 --> 00:14:44.660
MSCs view is that they
did try and do things

00:14:45.360 --> 00:14:47.850
that would help curb this problem.

00:14:47.850 --> 00:14:50.700 position:64%
Some cases, they put guards on some ships

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:53.610
going from the east coast
of South America to Europe.

00:14:53.610 --> 00:14:57.540
They tried to find other
ways which they could

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.690
secure those logistic
lines from traffickers.

00:15:00.690 --> 00:15:03.180
But their point mainly
is that we were doing

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:06.660
everything that every other
shipping company was doing.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:09.720
There's an ongoing investigation
based out of New York

00:15:09.720 --> 00:15:14.720
into the Balkan cartel and
key individuals in the cartel.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.180
Recently, there was a
breakthrough arrest in Miami.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.520
This individual was a
former heavyweight boxer

00:15:20.520 --> 00:15:23.550
from the Balkans, and
according to the indictment,

00:15:23.550 --> 00:15:26.370
he played a major coordinating role

00:15:26.370 --> 00:15:30.226
helping to recruit
sailors aboard MSC ships

00:15:30.226 --> 00:15:32.490
and there are eight
crewmen who were charged

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:36.240
and ultimately pled guilty and
are in federal prisons now.

00:15:36.240 --> 00:15:38.640 position:63%
- After the crew members were sentenced,

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:42.360
it turned out the case
wasn't entirely over.

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.300 position:64%
There was still the lingering question of

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.770
what responsibility does
MSC have in any of this?

00:15:49.770 --> 00:15:52.560 position:61%
And it turns out that there's a statute

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:57.030
that says essentially that
if you're caught with any

00:15:57.030 --> 00:16:00.030
illicit cargo, in particular,
if you're caught with cocaine,

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:04.110 position:66%
you will be fined 1,000 dollars per ounce.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:06.780 position:64%
Customs and border protection authorities

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:09.930
added up all the cocaine,
the 20 tons of cocaine,

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:13.830
multiplied it by the
1,000 dollars per kilo,

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:16.530 position:66%
and came up with what their fine would be.

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.750
It was over 600 million dollars.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:22.860
The largest fine ever assessed
against a commercial carrier.

00:16:22.860 --> 00:16:25.920 position:58%
The Gayane was seized by US officials

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.570
during the course of the search

00:16:27.570 --> 00:16:31.080
and was detained for over a month.

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:33.630
- The Gayane is worth at least
a hundred million dollars

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:35.190
but it's also, once you seize it,

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:38.220
you take one of these massive
assets out of the lineup

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:40.230 position:63%
of cargo ships that the company is using

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:41.640
to move goods around the world.

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:44.040 position:66%
So it was a pretty shocking event for MSC.

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.580
They reached an agreement
with US authorities

00:16:47.580 --> 00:16:49.140
to pay 50 million dollars,

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:52.620
which is essentially putting
the ship out on bail.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.870 position:61%
There's a civil case going on right now

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:57.870
where there's an effort
by US authorities to show

00:16:57.870 --> 00:17:01.020
that that initial seizure
should turn into a forfeiture.

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.510 position:64%
In other words, MSC should lose the ship.

00:17:03.510 --> 00:17:06.120 position:64%
MSC is arguing that they're not culpable.

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:08.850
US government is arguing
that in fact they are

00:17:08.850 --> 00:17:13.440
and that case has the potential
to really change the map

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:15.450
for shipping companies.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.960
US authorities really want to
make an example of this case

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:22.230
for not just MSC, but the
shipping industry generally.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:25.200 position:63%
It's absolutely clear MSC wasn't itself,

00:17:25.200 --> 00:17:26.910
as a company, smuggling the drugs,

00:17:26.910 --> 00:17:29.160 position:66%
the Balkan cartel was smuggling the drugs.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.640
But there's a question about
how the company behaves

00:17:32.640 --> 00:17:35.940
as its infrastructure is being used

00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:38.970
and misused by drug trafficking.

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.830
- Even the most amount of money

00:17:40.830 --> 00:17:43.170 position:66%
that a shipping company can spend on this,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:45.180
on guards, and on cameras,

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:48.090
and on other technology
like smart containers

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.490 position:64%
that can detect when a container is open,

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:52.110
even throwing money at that,

00:17:52.110 --> 00:17:55.050
it's gonna be very difficult
to make a dent in this problem

00:17:55.050 --> 00:18:00.050
because you still have an
enormous trade in cocaine.

00:18:00.063 --> 00:18:03.240
- Even though there were 20
tons of cocaine on that ship,

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:05.817
it was just a fraction
of the overall cargo

00:18:05.817 --> 00:18:09.870
and the cocaine was found in
seven different containers.

00:18:09.870 --> 00:18:12.570 position:64%
But that's seven containers out of 4,000.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:15.170
It's a really, really,
really hard problem to solve.

